WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.070
Your payments collection lives in one replica set.

2
00:00:06.070 --> 00:00:10.249
Its disk is almost full, and one primary handles every change.

3
00:00:10.249 --> 00:00:14.496
More secondaries don't help: each one replays every change too.

4
00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:17.991
So you split the collection across two replica sets.

5
00:00:17.991 --> 00:00:19.257
Each one is a shard.

6
00:00:19.467 --> 00:00:23.004
The split follows a shard key, here the payment id.

7
00:00:23.004 --> 00:00:29.315
MongoDB cuts the key's values into ranges called chunks, and each chunk lives on one shard.

8
00:00:29.533 --> 00:00:35.129
The config servers, a replica set of their own, keep this map of which range lives where.

9
00:00:35.333 --> 00:00:37.400
Your app never talks to a shard.

10
00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:41.661
It talks to mongos, a router that keeps a cached copy of that map.

11
00:00:41.867 --> 00:00:43.627
Find payment pay-81002.

12
00:00:43.627 --> 00:00:50.669
The filter has the shard key, so the router sends it only to the shard that owns that range.

13
00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:53.284
Find merchant M-0042's payments.

14
00:00:53.284 --> 00:00:59.543
That filter has no shard key, so the router asks every shard and merges the answers.

15
00:00:59.543 --> 00:01:01.779
That's a scatter-gather query.

16
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:03.627
Now new payments arrive.

17
00:01:03.627 --> 00:01:09.459
Ids only go up, so every insert lands in the last chunk, and one shard takes them all.

18
00:01:09.667 --> 00:01:19.807
When shard 2 holds 384 megabytes more, the balancer, on the config server primary, cuts a range off that chunk and moves it to shard 1.

19
00:01:20.033 --> 00:01:26.949
Shard 1 copies the documents, the config servers record the new owner, and shard 2 deletes its copy.

20
00:01:26.949 --> 00:01:29.647
But new payments still land on shard 2.

21
00:01:29.867 --> 00:01:32.102
A hashed shard key fixes that.

22
00:01:32.102 --> 00:01:34.188
MongoDB hashes the id first.

23
00:01:34.188 --> 00:01:40.148
Ids next to each other get far-apart hashes, so inserts spread over both shards.

24
00:01:40.367 --> 00:01:45.037
A lookup by id is still targeted, since the router can hash it too.

25
00:01:45.037 --> 00:01:47.825
Only a range of ids goes to every shard.

26
00:01:48.033 --> 00:01:54.123
So pick a shard key that your busiest reads filter on, with many values and no steady growth.

27
00:01:54.123 --> 00:01:58.052
mongos routes by it, and the balancer keeps the shards even.
